<h1>If You Are in Crisis</h1>
<p>This is an educational platform. <strong>It cannot provide crisis support, and messages
here are not monitored in real time.</strong> If you are in immediate danger or thinking
about harming yourself, please contact local emergency services right now, or use one of the
helplines below.</p>
<p>You matter. Reaching out is a reasonable, common, and serious step — not a failure.</p>
<p><strong>Last updated: 2026-10-23</strong></p>

<h2>Immediate options</h2>
<p>If you are in immediate danger or thinking about harming yourself:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Call your country's emergency number.</strong> Examples: 120 (medical) or 110 (police) in China; 911 in the United States and Canada; 112 in much of Europe; 999 in the United Kingdom.</li>
  <li><strong>Go to a hospital emergency department</strong>, or ask someone nearby to stay with you.</li>
  <li><strong>Do not be alone</strong> if you can help it. Move to a place with other people.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Helplines by country or region</h2>
<p>Helplines are staffed, free, and confidential in most cases. <strong>Phone numbers can
change; please verify against an official source before relying on one.</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>United States:</strong> 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline — call or text <strong>988</strong> (24/7, free, confidential). <code>https://988lifeline.org</code></li>
  <li><strong>United Kingdom &amp; Republic of Ireland:</strong> Samaritans — call <strong>116 123</strong> (free, 24/7). <code>https://www.samaritans.org</code></li>
  <li><strong>Australia:</strong> Lifeline — call <strong>13 11 14</strong> (24/7). <code>https://www.lifeline.org.au</code></li>
  <li><strong>New Zealand:</strong> call or text <strong>1737</strong> (free, 24/7). <code>https://1737.org.nz</code></li>
  <li><strong>Canada:</strong> call or text <strong>988</strong> (24/7). <code>https://988.ca</code></li>
  <li><strong>Anywhere:</strong> Find a helpline in your country at <strong>findahelpline.com</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What happens when you call a helpline</h2>
<p>Knowing what to expect can make the first call easier:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A trained responder answers; you do not need to have a "good enough" reason.</li>
  <li>You can talk as much or as little as you want.</li>
  <li>In most cases the call is free and confidential.</li>
  <li>If you are in immediate danger, the responder can help you connect to emergency services.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When to reach out</h2>
<p>You do not need to be at your lowest point to reach out. Consider contacting a helpline
or professional if you notice any of the following:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Thoughts of harming yourself or ending your life.</li>
  <li>Feeling that things would be better if you were not here.</li>
  <li>Overwhelming distress that feels unmanageable.</li>
  <li>A significant change in mood, sleep, or appetite that concerns you.</li>
  <li>Feeling trapped or without hope.</li>
</ul>
<p>Reaching out early is common and sensible, not a sign of weakness.</p>

<h2>Not in crisis, but struggling</h2>
<p>If pornography use or related distress is affecting your life, consider speaking with a
qualified professional — a doctor, psychologist, counselor, or therapist. This platform
explains the evidence; it does not treat. A professional can offer individualized assessment
and support that a website cannot.</p>
<p>For general questions about whether and how to seek help, see
<a href="/problems/should-i-see-a-therapist/">Should I see a therapist?</a>.</p>

<h2>What this platform is and is not</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>This site is not a crisis service.</strong> It is not staffed, and it is not monitored in real time.</li>
  <li><strong>This site does not provide medical or psychological advice.</strong> See the <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>This site does not treat, coach, or counsel.</strong> If you need support, use the resources above or contact a qualified professional.</li>
</ul>

<h2>If you are worried about someone else</h2>
<p>If someone you know is in crisis:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Stay with them if you can, and remove immediate means of harm where safe to do so.</li>
  <li>Encourage them to contact a helpline or emergency services.</li>
  <li>If they are in immediate danger, call emergency services yourself.</li>
  <li>Offer to sit with them while they make the call, if that helps.</li>
</ul>
<p>You do not have to solve it alone, and you do not have to be perfect — being present and
connecting them to help is enough. If you are supporting someone else, look after your own
wellbeing too.</p>

<h2>A note on language and location</h2>
<p>Crisis services vary by country, and many are available in multiple languages. If the
numbers above do not match your country, use <strong>findahelpline.com</strong> to find a
verified service in your region and language.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<p><strong>Will a helpline tell anyone I called?</strong> In most cases calls are confidential. Ask the responder about their confidentiality policy if you are concerned.</p>
<p><strong>What if I can't afford a therapist?</strong> Many regions offer low-cost or free mental-health services. A helpline can often point you to local options.</p>
<p><strong>Is it okay to call if I'm not sure it's "serious enough"?</strong> Yes. Helplines exist for people at every level of distress.</p>
<p><strong>What if I'm worried about someone who won't get help?</strong> Be present, encourage them, and if there is immediate danger, contact emergency services.</p>

<p><em>Last updated: 2026-10-23.</em></p>
</BaseLayout>
